age of listening upfront is that  it gives you momentum for your reading  because when we read in a foreign  language we tend to sub vocalize so we  have to know how the words are  pronounced and we don't want to rely at  least I can't rely on some kind of  phonetic or you know and particularly in  a language where the writing system is  not terribly phonetic  I need the assist of the listening and  in fact we know that good reading skills  come from good listening skills people  who have trouble reading even in their  own language are people who didn't hear  a lot of the language when they were  young or who have trouble hearing  hearing good hearing good listening  leads to good reading good reading is  where you pick up Andrew lots of reading  is where you pick up the all important  words and phrases and you start to  notice them more and more you noticed it  once here and once you've noticed it  once in some listening or in some  reading you tend to notice it somewhere  else and then you load it notice it  again while listening and so there's a  sort of a virtuous circle of exposing  yourself to so much content that you  start to notice things of course a  teacher may point certain things out to  you or you may notice by listening to if  I listen to myself speak Portuguese I  noticed the mistakes that I make and  that helps me to notice again and all of  this is giving the brain a chance to get  used to the language because the brain  is not very good at absorbing  theoretical explanations the brain is  much better at creating linkages  recognizing patterns as it gradually  gets used to a new language for the  brain to do that because the brain is  good at doing it but it does it slowly  you have to feed the brain a lot of  input listening and reading now in that  listening and reading it's important to  have a strategy this is another basic  concept that I think is very important  you want to vary the repetition with the  novelty you  cannot just push at the br